<h1>Terrain assets and editing</h1>
<p>A Terrain asset owns its generation graph, biome settings and manual edits. Scenes reference it through <code>Terrain3D.asset</code> and place it with Transform 3D. The editor generates and edits its preview; shipped games load prepared terrain data. This is an experimental finite-heightfield workflow; large-world streaming and volumetric caves are not yet available.</p>

<h2>Generate a terrain</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Choose <strong>Assets → Create → Terrain...</strong> to create a Terrain asset. Double-click its source or manifest to open its own terrain stage, independently of the scene.</li>
  <li>Choose <strong>Open Terrain Graph</strong>. Its generation settings contain the X/Z size in world units, resolution in cells, seed and chunk size. Resolution determines surface detail; chunk size controls how the surface is split into meshes.</li>
  <li>Adjust landforms, biomes, rules, palette and connected graph modules there, then press <strong>Generate</strong> or <strong>Regenerate</strong>. Settings remain an unapplied draft until you do this.</li>
  <li>Inspect the result in the viewport. Generation runs in the background with progress and cancellation. The last completed surface remains visible while it runs.</li>
  <li>Save the asset normally. Undo/Redo restores its authored recipe and preview. Drag the asset from Assets into a 3D scene to place it; the scene stores a reference, not a recipe copy.</li>
</ol>
<p>An empty Terrain component is a placeholder until an asset or an explicit procedural recipe is assigned. Terrain-local X/Z coordinates run from zero to the configured size; use the scene entity's transform to position it. Generated meshes and static triangle-mesh colliders are split into chunks and biome groups. One logical Terrain asset therefore has multiple native render/collision objects.</p>
<p>Select the terrain owner and press <strong>F</strong> over the viewport to frame its full surface. Generated chunks are hidden from the authored hierarchy. Generation configuration belongs to the Terrain Graph workspace; the Terrain component Inspector contains the viewport brush tools.</p>

<h2>Edit a placed terrain</h2>
<p>Select a scene entity with a <code>Terrain3D.asset</code> reference. Sculpt and paint it directly in the scene Viewport, using the Terrain component's brush controls. There is no need to open an isolated asset stage. <strong>Open Terrain Graph</strong> edits the same shared asset's generation draft.</p>
<p>Use <strong>Apply changes to asset</strong> to save the edited recipe and prepared surface. Edits affect other placements of that asset; their transforms remain independent. The scene continues to store its asset reference, without embedding an override recipe. Duplicate the source asset first if a placement needs a different terrain. The standalone Terrain asset stage remains available for working in isolation.</p>
<p>Selecting an unrelated scene object does not discard pending terrain edits. Before editing a different Terrain asset, apply or undo the current asset's changes and generate or discard its pending settings. Scene placement edits and terrain source edits have separate histories.</p>

<h2>Terrain module graph</h2>
<p>Try <code>demi editor --project examples/terrain_graph_3d</code> for a connected landscape example: mountain, foothill and lowland noise branches blend through elevation masks, followed by drainage-fed erosion, lake/river carving and biome rules. Its terrain can then be painted and sculpted in the Viewport. The water nodes retain surface data; visible water rendering remains pending.</p>
<p>The graph lives in the Terrain asset's <code>recipe.graph</code>. Select its scene placement or open the asset, then choose <strong>Open Terrain Graph</strong> in the Inspector. Global recipe settings and node parameters are edited in this workspace, with one authoritative recipe rather than separate Inspector and graph copies. Use <strong>Starter graph</strong> for a working chain, or drag terrain cards from the independent <strong>Terrain Nodes</strong> panel onto the canvas. Each card has a stable type; moving a node changes layout, while connections and parameters determine generation.</p>
<p>Opening the graph pins it to that terrain's stable owner and source document. Changing Inspector selection does not switch the graph to another terrain or discard its draft. Graph shortcuts also work from Terrain Nodes. To open another terrain's graph, generate or discard the current draft and finish any pending generation or brush stroke first. Select the bound terrain when using its Viewport brushes; editing the graph does not force scene selection back to that terrain.</p>
<p>Connect an output pin to an input pin of the same kind. Pins carry a heightfield, mask, drainage result, water result or instance placements. An input accepts one connection, cycles and incompatible links are rejected, and required inputs must be connected before generation. The graph names the <strong>Terrain Output</strong> node by its stable node ID; an unconnected or invalid draft shows diagnostics. You can keep assembling an incomplete draft, use <strong>Undo graph</strong> or <strong>Redo graph</strong>, and press <strong>Generate</strong> when it is ready. Generation validates and evaluates the draft before replacing the committed terrain; the previous surface stays available if generation fails.</p>
<p>The registry currently provides these modules:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Generators:</strong> Landform uses the recipe's landscape settings; Noise terrain makes a seeded heightfield; Flat terrain makes a constant-height base.</li>
  <li><strong>Masks:</strong> Elevation mask and Slope mask select height or steepness bands. Connect a mask to Height Blend or Height Offset to limit its effect.</li>
  <li><strong>Modifiers:</strong> Height Blend mixes two heightfields; Height Offset raises or lowers one. Their inputs must share the same terrain grid.</li>
  <li><strong>Simulation:</strong> Drainage computes flow and basins for a surface; Erosion changes its heights and can take matching drainage as an optional input.</li>
  <li><strong>Appearance and placement:</strong> Biome Rules assigns rule-based biomes to its connected field; painted region overrides are applied after graph evaluation. Scatter Palette generates deterministic instances from a resolved asset palette.</li>
  <li><strong>Water and output:</strong> Water Body carves a finite lake, ocean or authored river path and produces a heightfield plus water data. Terrain Output publishes the connected heightfield and optional water and instances.</li>
</ul>
<p>The graph produces the procedural base. The terrain's authored regions, sculpt edits, exclusions and protection stay in separate surface layers and are replayed when you Generate again; they do not turn into graph nodes. A size or resolution change with existing edits opens the editor's keep-or-clear decision, and protection snapshots cannot be moved to a different grid. Water Body computes carved terrain and water-surface data for finite authored bodies; the current terrain renderer does not draw those water surfaces.</p>

<p>When chaining Water Body nodes, connect both the terrain output and the earlier node's water output to the next node's <strong>Existing water</strong> input. This preserves the earlier bodies while carving the new one. Connect the final water result to Terrain Output. Biome Rules currently derives its water context from the recipe's landforms; graph-authored bodies do not automatically replace that context.</p>
<p>Scroll the mouse wheel over the canvas to zoom around the cursor; middle-drag pans. The zoom percentage is view state, not part of the terrain recipe. <strong>100%</strong> resets the scale without changing authored node coordinates.</p>
<p>Terrain Nodes uses cards with an icon, title and description. Drag any part of a card onto the graph canvas and release to insert one node at that position; hovering does not change the graph. Dropping accounts for canvas zoom and panning, and insertion can be undone. The optional local node palette uses the same cards and also supports click-to-insert.</p>
<h3>Configure connections and the active output</h3>
<p>Each node separates <strong>Inputs</strong>, <strong>Parameters</strong> and <strong>Outputs</strong>. An input's source dropdown lists compatible outputs from other nodes. Choose a source to connect or replace a wire; choose <strong>None</strong> to disconnect it. Replacing a connection is one Undo step. Required inputs must be connected before Generate. Invalid cycles are rejected without changing the draft.</p>
<p>On a Terrain Output node, select <strong>Active terrain output</strong> to publish that branch. A node's port types are defined by its operation: a terrain surface connects to a terrain-surface input, while water and instance results use their matching inputs.</p>
